 "transcript": [
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "My Samsung dishwasher DW60M6050FS keeps showing 4C and beeping, won't do anything"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "The '4C' code on your Samsung dishwasher indicates a water supply issue – specifically that the machine is not receiving enough fresh water to start its cycle. This usually happens because the tap isn't fully open, the inlet hose is kinked, or the small filter at the water inlet is blocked with debris. To fix this, please check if the water tap behind the dishwasher is fully open. Then, unplug the machine and disconnect the inlet hose from the back of the dishwasher to inspect the small mesh filter inside the machine's inlet port for blockages. Let me know what you find."
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "Ok. Under the sink there's a valve with a screwdriver slot and the slot is crosswise to the pipe, at 90 degrees. The hose has no kinks and the water pressure at the kitchen tap is fine. Should I turn that valve?"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "Yes, turn that valve so the slot is parallel to the pipe (open). Then unplug the machine, disconnect the inlet hose from the back of the dishwasher and check the small mesh filter inside the machine's inlet port for debris. Let me know if it starts filling after opening the valve."
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "I turned the valve so the slot is parallel to the pipe and it's filling now and running normally. Do I still need to check the inlet filter?"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "No, you don't need to check the inlet filter now. You can leave it as is. If this happens again in the future, just double-check that the valve under the sink hasn't been accidentally bumped closed."
  }
 ],
